{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"All four questions have concrete answers in the code, and two of them change the plan.\n\n**1. Token estimates — you're right, and it's worse than you think.** `estimateTokens` (`src/transcript.ts:63`) is literally `Math.ceil(text.length / 4)`. It's a byte count wearing a token costume. Turnbridge never tokenizes anything — the ledger stores text blocks, fabricated files are raw text — so that number is model-agnostic in the bad way: it implies a precision that doesn't survive a tokenizer change, and it's the *source* model's rough shape being reported to the user about a *target* model's context. I'll delete it and report raw size (characters / KB) instead. That's a true statement about the artifact and sidesteps tokenizer transferability entirely.\n\n**2. Monotonicity — no recording bug; it's one line we write ourselves.** The timestamps originate from the source CLI's own transcript (`timestamp` on each Claude line / Codex rollout line), and cledger copies them verbatim into `occurred_at` (`adapters/claude-code.js:143`, `adapters/codex.js:172`). The history is in order and always was. What breaks it is the **import-notice line turnbridge synthesizes at the top** of every fabricated file: we stamp that with `now` (today), then every real turn below it carries its original timestamp (last week). So the file reads July 27 → July 21 → July 21 → … — exactly one step backward, at line 1, caused by us. Nothing to do with sorting across CLIs. Fix is to stamp the notice 1ms before the first real event.\n\n**3. Tool history — cledger records it, and we keep it in one direction but not the other.**\n- Codex→**Claude**: structured. `convertBlocks` (`claude-code.ts:77-99`) emits real `tool_use`/`tool_result` blocks, and Claude accepts foreign tool names verbatim (verified in the spec).\n- →**Codex**: folded to labeled text. `foldBlock` (`codex.ts`) turns a tool call into `[used tool: Bash]\\ninput: {...}`.\n\nThe data isn't missing and the CLIs aren't dropping it from their transcripts — the loss is ours, at fabricate time, for the Codex target only. The reason is defensive: the spec could only verify that plain `message` response_items replay safely; `function_call`/`custom_tool_call` replay is unverified and a bad guess could break the whole session. So **G6 isn't really a feature — it's a fidelity asymmetry**, and I'm revising my earlier call. I'd include it, with the caveat that it's the item most capable of breaking a session, so it gets tested in a scratch repo before it goes near the default path.\n\n**4. Context length — we do nothing, and your instinct is the right design.** Turnbridge passes the entire conversation naively. The only context-related behavior anywhere is that one stderr note, and it fires **only on the bootstrap path** — the default fabrication path says nothing. Both targets do have their own machinery: Claude Code auto-compacts as context fills, and Codex has a `compacted` rollout line type that the spec documents as appearing \"when Codex auto-compacts long history.\"\n\nWhat I genuinely don't know — and won't guess — is how either behaves on a **fabricated file loaded all at once at session start** versus history that grew organically. That's the real G1 question, and it's a better question than \"where's the byte cliff.\" So G1 becomes: confirm the target's own compaction/error handling engages gracefully on a large import, at your capped size. If it does, we keep passing naively and warn about nothing.\n\n**Revised scope:** G1–G5 as amended, G7–G9 as approved, G6 included per the above. Two calls I'll make by default unless you say otherwise: size reporting becomes characters/KB (not tokens) and stays a note rather than a block, and G6 ships behind verification — if `function_call` replay fails the probe, Codex keeps text folding and I document it as confirmed-unsafe rather than unverified.\n\nStarting on the settled items now — t